\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Mechanism behind low-rank spectral bias. Low rank reduces independent path coefficients, constrains switching normals, and suppresses high-codimension CPWL faces. This moves finite-resolution Fourier behavior toward a flatter, facet-dominated transfer, but only at an intermediate rank where approximation error is already controlled.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanism-summary}{{2}{7}{Mechanism behind low-rank spectral bias. Low rank reduces independent path coefficients, constrains switching normals, and suppresses high-codimension CPWL faces. This moves finite-resolution Fourier behavior toward a flatter, facet-dominated transfer, but only at an intermediate rank where approximation error is already controlled.\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
\newlabel{eq:recovery}{{22}{7}{Face Fourier expansion}{equation.4.22}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}Experiments}{7}{section.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:experiments}{{5}{7}{Experiments}{section.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Theory proxy for the Fourier shell law. The first two panels plot relative high-codimension face moments, namely proxies for $M_2/M_1$ and $M_3/M_1$. Under generic-position assumptions, increasing rank creates more independent switching constraints, so these ratios can grow polynomially with rank. The third panel translates this into the shell law: when high-codimension terms are suppressed, the finite-shell slope stays closer to the facet-dominated $\rho ^{-4}$ energy regime; as rank grows, higher-codimension contributions make the effective slope more negative.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:theory-proxy-main}{{3}{8}{Theory proxy for the Fourier shell law. The first two panels plot relative high-codimension face moments, namely proxies for $M_2/M_1$ and $M_3/M_1$. Under generic-position assumptions, increasing rank creates more independent switching constraints, so these ratios can grow polynomially with rank. The third panel translates this into the shell law: when high-codimension terms are suppressed, the finite-shell slope stays closer to the facet-dominated $\rho ^{-4}$ energy regime; as rank grows, higher-codimension contributions make the effective slope more negative.\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
\newlabel{eq:rank-risk}{{26}{8}{Experiments}{equation.5.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional CPWL geometry proxy. Theory predicts that increasing rank creates more linear regions but also more independent switching constraints, so multi-region junctions, especially the high-codimension proxy in Panel A, become more frequent. The ``empirical fraction'' axis is not a Fourier frequency: it is the fraction of sampled grid locations whose local neighborhood behaves like a multi-region junction. Panel C checks expressivity through the number of linear regions. Panel D estimates the shell-averaged Fourier slope: as high-codimension moments grow, the effective finite-shell slope becomes more negative; reducing rank suppresses these intersections and flattens the spectrum until approximation becomes limiting.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cpwl3d-main}{{4}{9}{Three-dimensional CPWL geometry proxy. Theory predicts that increasing rank creates more linear regions but also more independent switching constraints, so multi-region junctions, especially the high-codimension proxy in Panel A, become more frequent. The ``empirical fraction'' axis is not a Fourier frequency: it is the fraction of sampled grid locations whose local neighborhood behaves like a multi-region junction. Panel C checks expressivity through the number of linear regions. Panel D estimates the shell-averaged Fourier slope: as high-codimension moments grow, the effective finite-shell slope becomes more negative; reducing rank suppresses these intersections and flattens the spectrum until approximation becomes limiting.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Rank-shift control. The best rank is task-dependent: broadening the target spectrum or increasing the finite-budget weight on high frequencies moves the optimum from the baseline value to much larger ranks, up to about $20$ or $40$ in the strongest controls. Horizontal dashed colored lines show the corresponding full-rank MSE baselines for each setting. The Sobolev-weighted setting means that the loss gives larger weight to high-frequency modes, as in Sobolev or Fourier training objectives; this makes additional rank useful for recovering oscillatory components.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rank-shift-control}{{5}{9}{Rank-shift control. The best rank is task-dependent: broadening the target spectrum or increasing the finite-budget weight on high frequencies moves the optimum from the baseline value to much larger ranks, up to about $20$ or $40$ in the strongest controls. Horizontal dashed colored lines show the corresponding full-rank MSE baselines for each setting. The Sobolev-weighted setting means that the loss gives larger weight to high-frequency modes, as in Sobolev or Fourier training objectives; this makes additional rank useful for recovering oscillatory components.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
