\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Mode-wise recovery and training curves. The recovery ratio is $R_r(k,t)=|\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle f$}\mathaccent "0362{f}_{r,t}(k)|/|\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle f$}\mathaccent "0362{f}^\star (k)|$: values near one mean that a Fourier mode is recovered, while values near zero mean that it is missing. The legend reports the fitted log-log recovery slope $\beta $ for each displayed curve. A steep negative slope indicates that high frequencies are much less recovered than low frequencies. The theory predicts the direction of these finite-resolution slopes through the face moments $M_q$ and spectral transfer coefficients, but not a universal numerical slope independent of task, budget, and frequency window.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:recovery-training-main}{{6}{10}{Mode-wise recovery and training curves. The recovery ratio is $R_r(k,t)=|\widehat f_{r,t}(k)|/|\widehat f^\star (k)|$: values near one mean that a Fourier mode is recovered, while values near zero mean that it is missing. The legend reports the fitted log-log recovery slope $\beta $ for each displayed curve. A steep negative slope indicates that high frequencies are much less recovered than low frequencies. The theory predicts the direction of these finite-resolution slopes through the face moments $M_q$ and spectral transfer coefficients, but not a universal numerical slope independent of task, budget, and frequency window.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
